import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/GuardLimits
import ../../../Demos/GuardPlayground
import ../../../Demos/GuardClients
import ../../../Demos/GuardFlags
import ../../../Demos/GuardCheckout
import ../../../Demos/GuardHeaders
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Demo

## The guard package: rate limits, feature flags and small request guards, as middleware. Its
## tables are the package's own answers, worked out by the server when the site is built; one
## demo runs a rate limit in the browser, with a clock moved by hand.
meta = {
	title: "Guard",
	description: "Rate limits, feature flags and request guards for Roc Solid apps, as middleware, decided by plain Roc that a test runs with a fake clock.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "first", label: "A first guard" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Rate limits" },
		{ id: "try", label: "Try a limit" },
		{ id: "proxies", label: "Behind a proxy" },
		{ id: "flags", label: "Feature flags" },
		{ id: "guards", label: "Other guards" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/guard</code>]}
				title="Rate limits, feature flags and guards, as middleware"
			>
				A login form takes 5 tries a minute from each address. A new checkout goes to staff first, then
				to a quarter of everyone. Every answer carries security headers, and the whole site can go into
				maintenance from an admin page. Each guard is middleware: it runs around a request, and lets it
				go on or refuses it. What it decides is plain Roc, so the tables on this page are the package's
				own answers, worked out when the site was built.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="first">A first guard</Heading>
			<p>
				A guard is <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a>. The app's default middleware
				runs around every request, and a route file names its own, which runs around that route. Here
				every answer gets security headers, a body over a megabyte is refused, the site can be put into
				maintenance, everything under <code>/api</code> is limited for each user, and the login route
				for each address:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_middlewares.file} source={Sources.guard_middlewares.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_route.file} source={Sources.guard_route.rocx} />
			<p>
				The sixth try in a minute is answered <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>, with
				<code>Retry-After</code> and the <code>RateLimit-*</code> headers. Every guard hands on the
				context it is given, unchanged, so guards go anywhere in a chain.
			</p>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header, as you name <a href={Paths.ecosystem_charts}>charts</a>:
				<code>{"guard: \"../packages/guard/main.roc\""}</code>, and import the middleware from it
				(<code>import guard.Guard</code>): the package names the server platform, and the browser's half
				of an app sees its logic alone. The code above is from <code>packages/guard/example/</code>, a
				whole app that builds and runs, and that its tests ask over HTTP.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Rate limits</Heading>
			<p>
				A limit says how many requests a client may send, and in how long.
				<code>RateLimit.per_minute(5)</code> is a token bucket: a client may send 5 at once, then one
				every 12 seconds. <code>.sliding()</code> counts the same limit in a sliding window: never more
				than 5 in any minute. Both are plain functions. A client's state and the time go in; whether the
				request may go on, and the next state, come out.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_limits.file} source={Sources.guard_limits.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Here are the same eleven requests under each limit, as <code>RateLimit.check</code> answered them
				when this page was built. The clock is a list of seconds, as it is in the package's tests.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardLimits />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The bucket lets the first five through, refuses the sixth, and gives a token back every 12
				seconds. A refused request costs nothing, so hammering doesn't push the next allowed one back.
				The sliding window refuses everything after the fifth until the five weigh less: a window's count
				is this minute's requests plus the last minute's, weighted by how much of it the window still
				covers. So it needs two numbers a client, however high the limit.
			</p>

			<Heading id="try">Try a limit</Heading>
			<p>
				The same <code>RateLimit.check</code>, in your browser, with a clock you move by hand. Nothing
				is sent anywhere: each line is what the server's middleware would answer.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="guard-limit"><GuardPlayground /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<details>
				<summary>The demo: <code>{Sources.guard_playground.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.guard_playground.file} source={Sources.guard_playground.rocx} />
			</details>

			<h3>Who is counted</h3>
			<p>
				<code>Guard.rate_limit(name, limit, key)</code> counts each key apart. <code>name</code> keeps one
				limit's counts apart from another's (<code>"login"</code>, <code>"api"</code>).
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Counts</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.ip(trust)</code></td><td>each address, as <a href="#proxies">trust</a> says to find it; an IPv6 address by its first 64 bits, the block one household is given</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.user(|context| context.user, trust)"}</code></td><td>each signed-in user, as the context names them; each address when signed out</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.route</code></td><td>each method and path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.both(key, key)</code></td><td>each pair: <code>Guard.both(Guard.ip(trust), Guard.route)</code> is each address on each route</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"|request, context| …"}</code></td><td>whatever it says. A request whose key is <code>""</code> isn't limited</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<h3>What a client is told</h3>
			<p>
				An allowed request's answer says where the client stands: <code>RateLimit-Limit</code>,
				<code>RateLimit-Remaining</code>, <code>RateLimit-Reset</code> (seconds until the whole limit is
				back) and <code>RateLimit-Policy</code> (<code>5;w=60</code>: 5 in 60 seconds). A refused one is
				a <code>429</code> with those, <code>Retry-After</code> (seconds until one more would be allowed,
				exactly), and a line of text.
			</p>

			<h3>Memory stays bounded</h3>
			<p>
				Each limit keeps at most 10,000 clients (<code>Guard.rate_limit_with</code> says another number).
				A client whose limit is whole again is forgotten first, which changes nothing. Past the limit, the
				client seen longest ago is forgotten, and starts again. Each limit's clients are split into 64
				tallies, so a request reads and writes about 150 at most. The counts live in the server's memory
				(<code>Kv</code>): a request reads its tally and swaps the next one in, reading again when
				another request changed it first, so no hit is lost and nothing is written to Postgres. When a
				tally can't be changed, the request goes on, and the log says why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="proxies">Behind a proxy</Heading>
			<p>
				Most apps are served behind a proxy, which connects to the server for the client and adds the
				address it saw to <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>. A client can write anything it likes in that
				header too, so the header is read only as far as the app says its own proxies wrote it. Take one
				request: it came from 10.0.0.2, the app's proxy, and its <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> is
				<code>6.6.6.6, 198.51.100.4</code>. The client wrote the first address itself; the proxy added
				the second, the address it saw. Here is who the client is, by each way to trust, as
				<code>ClientIp.of</code> worked it out when this page was built:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardClients />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Hops(1)</code> says one proxy is in front, and finds the address it saw.
				<code>Hops(2)</code> claims two, so it trusts what the client wrote. <code>Proxies</code> walks
				the list from the right, past the app's own proxies (<code>Ip.private</code>: the loopback and
				private ranges, or <code>{"Ip.cidrs([\"10.0.0.0/8\"])"}</code>). <code>Header(name)</code> reads
				one header a proxy sets, such as <code>fly-client-ip</code> or <code>cf-connecting-ip</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				The table's request has the address it came from. The server platform doesn't hand that address to
				Roc yet, so today <code>Direct</code> can't tell clients apart (it gives no key, so no limit), and
				an app limits by address behind its proxy.
			</p>

			<Heading id="flags">Feature flags</Heading>
			<p>
				A flag is defined in code: its name, what it is for, and who has it on. A rule is on or off for
				everyone (off is a kill switch), plus the users and groups listed, plus a share of everyone else
				who is signed in. Each user falls in or out of a rollout the same way every time, by a hash of
				the flag's name and theirs, so raising the share only adds people. An environment can have a rule
				of its own.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_flag.file} source={Sources.guard_flag.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Here is who has it on, as <code>Flags.is_on</code> answered when this page was built. A user's
				bucket, 0 to 99, is that hash (<code>Flags.bucket</code>): a rollout of 25 takes those below 25.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardFlags />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				ada is listed and bea is in staff, so they have it whatever their bucket. jo and max fall in the
				quarter; cy, dee and kai don't. A visitor who isn't signed in has no id to be counted by, so a
				rollout reaches them only at 100.
				The environment is <code>APP_ENV</code> if it is set, or else <code>dev</code> when the server
				was started with <code>--dev</code>, or else <code>production</code>.
			</p>

			<h3>Changed while the server runs</h3>
			<p>
				What the code says is the default. An admin page can set a rule in its place, until it is reset.
				The server keeps it in its memory (<code>Kv</code>), so a restart brings back the code's rules.
				The endpoint that sets it is for admins only: in the example, <code>Middlewares.admins</code>
				answers anyone else <code>403</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_overrides.file} source={Sources.guard_overrides.rocx} />
			<p>
				On the server, <code>{"FlagStore.is_on!(flag, { user, groups })"}</code> answers in an endpoint,
				and <code>Guard.feature(flag, who)</code> keeps a route from anyone who doesn't have the flag on:
				they get a 404, as if it weren't there.
			</p>

			<h3>In a page</h3>
			<p>
				The server decides the flags once for the visitor, in a GET endpoint the layout reads, and a
				<code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> shows its children only when its flag is on. The server renders the
				page with the flags and puts them in it, so the browser hydrates with the same values, without
				asking again.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_visitor.file} source={Sources.guard_visitor.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_layout.file} source={Sources.guard_layout.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_page.file} source={Sources.guard_page.rocx} />
			<p>
				This site has no server to ask, so the example below decides the flags where the page is
				rendered, when the site is built. ada is listed for the new checkout; cy isn't, and isn't in the
				quarter the rollout reaches:
			</p>
			<div class="guard-checkouts">
				<GuardCheckout who={{ user: "ada", groups: [] }} />
				<GuardCheckout who={{ user: "cy", groups: [] }} />
			</div>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_checkout.file} source={Sources.guard_checkout.rocx} />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>{"<Flagged>"} prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td>the flag's name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>fallback : Html</code></td><td>what shows when the flag is off: nothing, unless given</td></tr>
						<tr><td>children</td><td>what shows when the flag is on</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"<FlagsContext value={…}>"}</code> provides the flags to every <code>{"<Flagged>"}</code>
				under it, and needs <code>import pf.Context</code>. Where nothing provides them, every flag is
				off. <code>Flags.enabled(values, name)</code> reads one flag where a <code>{"<Show>"}</code> reads
				better.
			</p>

			<Heading id="guards">Other guards</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Guard</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.security_headers(Security.defaults)</code></td><td>every answer with the headers below, but those it sets itself</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.body_limit(bytes)</code></td><td>a larger body refused, <code>413</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.methods([\"GET\", \"POST\"])"}</code></td><td>any other method refused, <code>405</code>, with <code>Allow</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.hosts([\"example.com\", \"*.example.com\"])"}</code></td><td>any other <code>Host</code> refused, <code>400</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.ips({ allow, deny, trust })"}</code></td><td>an address in <code>deny</code>, or not in <code>allow</code> when it lists any, refused, <code>403</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.maintenance({ flag, message, retry_after_s, except })"}</code></td><td>while the flag is on, <code>503</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> and a small page, except under the paths in <code>except</code> (<code>/admin</code>, to turn it off again)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.feature(flag, who)</code></td><td><code>404</code> for anyone who doesn't have the flag on</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.under(\"/api\", guard)"}</code></td><td><code>guard</code> for paths under <code>/api</code> only</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Security.defaults</code> adds these headers to every answer, as <code>Security.headers</code>
				wrote them when this page was built:
			</p>
			<Code title="Security.headers(Security.defaults, { dev: Bool.False })" source={GuardHeaders.text} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The Content-Security-Policy fits a Roc Solid app. Scripts and WebAssembly come from the site's
				own origin: a page needs <code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code> to start its wasm under a CSP. Styles may
				be inline too, as components write <code>{"style=\"…\""}</code>, and calls go to the site's own
				server. <code>Security.directive</code> changes one directive. Under <code>--dev</code>, there is
				no <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>, and the CSP only reports, so the dev server's reload
				script still runs.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				The package is plain Roc, on no platform, so the server, the browser and a test decide alike.
				<code>roc test packages/guard/main.roc</code> runs its <code>expect</code>s.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>RateLimit</code></td><td>a limit: <code>per_second</code>, <code>per_minute</code>, <code>per_hour</code>, <code>per(n, window_ms)</code>, <code>.sliding()</code>; <code>fresh</code>, <code>check(state, now)</code>, <code>idle</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>explain</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Tally</code></td><td>many clients' states by key, bounded: <code>Tally.hit(tally, limit, key, now, room)</code>; <code>to_str</code>, <code>from_str</code>, <code>shard</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ClientIp</code></td><td><code>ClientIp.of(trust, peer, headers)</code>, by <code>Direct</code>, <code>Header(name)</code>, <code>Hops(n)</code> or <code>Proxies(cidrs)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ip</code></td><td>IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and ranges: <code>parse</code>, <code>to_str</code>, <code>cidr</code>, <code>cidrs</code>, <code>within</code>, <code>within_any</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>private</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Flag</code></td><td>a flag in code: <code>Flag.define(name, about, rule)</code>, <code>.in_env(env, rule)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Flags</code></td><td>rules (<code>on</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>rollout(percent)</code>, <code>only(users, groups)</code>), <code>is_on</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>set</code> and <code>reset</code> for overrides, <code>describe</code> for an admin page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Security</code></td><td>security headers: <code>defaults</code>, <code>directives</code>, <code>directive</code>, <code>csp</code>, <code>headers</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hash</code></td><td>a small, stable hash of text, the same on every machine: <code>of</code>, and <code>bucket</code>, which puts a user in or out of a rollout and a client in a tally</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Checks</code></td><td>checks on a request: <code>methods</code>, <code>hosts</code>, <code>body_size</code>, <code>ips</code>, <code>maintenance</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The middleware is the package's server half (<code>Guard</code>, <code>FlagStore</code>,
				<code>Kept</code>), Roc that only the server runs.
				<code>Guard</code> holds the guards, and <code>FlagStore</code> holds the flags while the server
				runs:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_middleware_api.file} source={Sources.guard_middleware_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_flagstore_api.file} source={Sources.guard_flagstore_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>
				What works today, and is tested: every module of the package, by its <code>expect</code>s; and
				the middleware, in <code>packages/guard/example/</code>, which builds, runs and is tested over HTTP. Its login
				limit answered <code>429</code> on the sixth try, with the headers above; its admin endpoint
				answered <code>403</code> to anyone but an admin; and its page showed each user their checkout. Its
				flags' overrides, its feature route and its maintenance mode worked while it ran, and a page with
				<code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> hydrated in a browser under the default CSP.
			</p>
			<p>What waits on a proposal (<code>packages/guard/PROPOSALS.md</code>):</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Overrides that outlive a restart.</strong> The flags' overrides are in the server's
					memory, so a deploy brings back the code's rules. A plugin with a table could keep them.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				The server reads a request's body, up to 16 MiB, before any middleware runs, so
				<code>Guard.body_limit</code> refuses a large body after it has come, not before.
			</Note>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	.guard-checkouts {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: var(--space-4) 0;
	}

	.guard-checkouts p {
		margin: 0;
		padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--bg-raised);
	}
</style>
